<!--
  A second voice for /docs/concepts/, kept beside it for review. Same facts
  and the same verified Lead sample (concepts.meta.js); whichever reads
  better replaces the other, and this file goes.
-->
<script>
  import DocsShell from '../../components/DocsShell.mesa'

  export let data = null

  const sample = data?.samples ?? {}
  const toc = [
    { href: '#why', title: 'Why one file' },
    { href: '#lead', title: 'A lead, start to finish' },
    { href: '#code', title: 'Where your code goes' },
    { href: '#more', title: 'When you need more' },
    { href: '#access', title: 'Who gets to see what' },
    { href: '#change', title: 'When something changes' },
    { href: '#surfaces', title: 'One app, many places' },
    { href: '#remember', title: 'If you remember three things' },
  ]
</script>

<DocsShell nav={data?.nav} current="/docs/concepts/" {toc}>
<article id="top" class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs · Concepts · Draft</p>
  <h1>One file, and everything that grows from it.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This is the page we'd want you to read before anything else. It isn't long,
    and once it clicks, most of FrontierJS stops being something to look up and
    starts being something you can guess.
  </p>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why one file</h2>
    <p>
      Think about the last time you added a field to an app. You probably put it in
      the database, then in a type, then in a validator, then in a form, and maybe in
      an API doc if you were being good. Five places saying the same thing, and
      sooner or later one of them is wrong, usually the one you forgot.
    </p>
    <p>
      We got tired of that. So in a FrontierJS app you say it once, in
      <code>db/schema.lite</code>, and the rest of the app takes it from there.
      Change the file and everything that came from it changes with it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="lead">
    <h2>A lead, start to finish</h2>
    <p>
      Let's use one model for the whole page. Say you're tracking sales leads. Here's
      the entire definition:
    </p>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.LEAD}</pre>
    <p>
      Most of it reads the way you'd guess. A name between 1 and 120 characters. An
      email that has to look like one, is stored lowercase and can't repeat. A phone
      number that's encrypted. A status that starts at <code>new</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The last few lines are where it gets interesting. <code>@@gate</code> and
      <code>@@allow</code> say who can do what, and <code>@@transitions</code> says
      how a lead's status is allowed to move: you can contact a new lead, win one
      you've contacted, or lose one that's either. That's it. There's no way to jump
      a lead straight from <code>new</code> to <code>won</code>, because nobody wrote
      that path.
    </p>
    <p>
      From those eighteen lines you get the table and its migrations, validation
      that runs the same rule on the server and in the browser, the access checks,
      the status rules, TypeScript types, a JSON Schema, a default form and the tools
      an AI agent sees. You didn't write any of those, and you won't have to keep
      them in step.
    </p>
    <p>
      One thing the schema deliberately doesn't hold: what to do. No UI behavior, no
      business logic. It says what's true about a lead. Keeping it to that is what
      makes everything built on it predictable. If you want to watch it happen,
      <a href="/tour/derived/">Derived</a> on the tour shows one model and everything
      it produces.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="code">
    <h2>Where your code goes</h2>
    <p>
      An app has three layers, and we gave each one exactly one noun so you always
      know which file you're looking for:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Noun</th><th>For our lead</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Data</td><td><strong>Model</strong></td><td><code>model Lead</code>, which you query as <code>db.lead</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>API</td><td><strong>Service</strong></td><td><code>leads.service.ts</code>, served at <code>/api/leads</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>UI</td><td><strong>Resource</strong></td><td><code>Lead.mesa</code>, which exports <code>leads</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Here's the service and the resource for <code>Lead</code>. Yes, really, this is all of them:</p>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.SERVICE}</pre>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.RESOURCE}</pre>
    <p>
      That empty service already lists, reads, creates, updates and deletes leads.
      It turns away bad input, checks who's calling and filters the list so each
      person sees only what they're allowed to. The files stay this small until you
      have something to say that the model can't, and then that's the only thing
      in them.
    </p>
    <p>
      You'll also hear two more nouns: a <strong>Release</strong> is how the app
      ships, and a <strong>Suite</strong> is how it's tested. The
      <a href="/tour/walkthrough/">walkthrough</a> builds all three layers from one
      model if you'd like to see it end to end.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="more">
    <h2>When you need more</h2>
    <p>
      Sooner or later the defaults won't cover something. When that happens, we'd
      nudge you to reach for the smallest tool that does the job, roughly in this
      order:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Say it in the schema.</strong> If it's a rule about the data, it belongs there, and then every part of the app knows it.</li>
      <li><strong>Add a hook.</strong> Step into one service's calls, before or after the work happens.</li>
      <li><strong>Add a plugin.</strong> Give the whole app a new ability, like background jobs or an integration.</li>
      <li><strong>Swap a provider.</strong> Change what's behind a seam, like which service sends your mail.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The reason for the order: a rule in the schema is enforced everywhere at once,
      while a rule in a hook only holds for the calls that pass through it. Put a
      data rule in a hook and the next script that writes to the table won't know
      it exists.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="access">
    <h2>Who gets to see what</h2>
    <p>
      Access lives in the schema too, and it's checked right where the data is read
      and written. That matters more than it sounds. An API route, a background job,
      a one-off script and an AI agent all go through the same check, so there's no
      path where somebody forgot to add it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every caller lands somewhere on a ladder from 0 to 9, and
      <code>@@gate</code> says how high you need to be for each operation, in the
      order read, create, update, delete:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ladder">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>STRANGER</td><td>5</td><td>ADMINISTRATOR</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>VISITOR</td><td>6</td><td>OWNER</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>READER</td><td>7</td><td>SYSADMIN</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>CREATOR</td><td>8</td><td>SYSTEM, only through <code>asSystem()</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>USER</td><td>9</td><td>LOCKED, which nobody passes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      So for our lead, <code>@@gate(2.4.4.5)</code> means a reader can look, a
      signed-in user can create and edit, and only an administrator can delete.
      Someone who isn't signed in gets nothing. Then <code>@@allow</code> narrows
      reading further: it's built into the query, so when a user lists leads they
      only get back their own. And the encrypted phone number stays out of ordinary
      reads altogether.
    </p>
    <p>
      The browser gets a copy of these rules too, so a screen can hide a Delete
      button you can't use. That's a nicety, not the lock. The server checks either
      way.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your schema has no access rules at all, it behaves like a plain ORM and
      stays out of your way. Once you add your first one, though, a model with no
      gate is closed to everyone but the system. And <code>asSystem()</code>, the
      one way around a gate, is recorded every time it's used.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="change">
    <h2>When something changes</h2>
    <p>
      When a lead is updated, the update is announced once, and every screen that's
      showing that lead updates with it. That includes changes that never went
      through an API call, like a job or a script writing directly, because the
      announcement comes from the data rather than from whoever made the change.
    </p>
    <p>
      The upshot is that a screen never keeps its own copy of the data that it has
      to remember to refresh. It points at the data, and live updates are just how
      apps behave rather than something you add later.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same instinct runs through the rest of the request. Every step where one
      layer hands off to the next has exactly one place that does it: one place turns
      an error into an HTTP status, one place reads <code>?$limit=20</code> off the
      URL. When something comes out wrong, you have one place to look instead of
      five. <a href="/tour/seams/">The seams</a> follows one request through every
      handoff.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="surfaces">
    <h2>One app, many places</h2>
    <p>
      Open an app's folder and you'll see <code>db/</code>, then a folder for each
      place people reach the app from: <code>api/</code>, <code>web/</code> for the
      signed-in app, <code>site/</code> for a public site, and, if you need them,
      <code>widgets/</code>, <code>extension/</code>, <code>desktop/</code>,
      <code>mobile/</code> and <code>cli/</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every interface is written in Mesa, and each folder is just where it gets
      built for. So a component you write for the web reads the same in the
      terminal or on a phone. If two of them need the same plain code, it goes in
      <code>shared/</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="remember">
    <h2>If you remember three things</h2>
    <p>Everything on this page comes back to three ideas:</p>
    <ul class="axioms">
      <li><strong>Say it once.</strong> Every fact has one home, and everything else reads from it.</li>
      <li><strong>Call it one thing.</strong> <code>Lead</code>, <code>db.lead</code>, <code>/api/leads</code> and <code>leads</code> are the same name in each layer, so finding one finds the others.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it one owner.</strong> Every rule and every handoff lives in exactly one place.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When something in FrontierJS surprises you, it's usually one of these three
      doing its job.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="next">
    <p>
      <strong>Where to next?</strong> <a href="/docs/ecosystem/">Ecosystem</a> covers
      the packages around an app. <a href="/tour/">The tour</a> shows each idea
      running. And <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/VOCABULARY.md">Vocabulary</a>
      has every word we use with a specific meaning.
    </p>
  </footer>
</article>
</DocsShell>

